No. `platforms` and `requires` can’t select viewport sizes; every browser target has platform `web`.

To skip a spec on narrow screens, check the width in the test and call `test.skip`, for example:

```ts
test('desktop layout only', async ({ app, browser }) => {
  await app.open('/');
  test.skip(
    (await browser.evaluate(() => window.innerWidth)) < 768,
    'desktop layout only',
  );
  // desktop-width assertions
});
```

Alternatively, put size-specific tests in a separate config and run it with `--config`. You can also define targets at different viewport sizes, but every test runs on every target; use `--target` to run only one target.

No. `platforms` and `requires` can’t select by viewport: every browser target has platform `web`.

To skip a spec on narrow screens, check the width in the test and call `test.skip`, for example:

```ts
test('desktop layout only', async ({ app, browser }) => {
  await app.open('/');
  test.skip(
    (await browser.evaluate(() => window.innerWidth)) < 768,
    'desktop layout only',
  );

  // Desktop-width assertions
});
```

Alternatively, put size-specific tests in a separate config and run it with `--config`. You can also define targets with different viewport sizes and run a particular target with `--target`.
